			<div class="content" id="post-content-2466054">
									

<blockquote>
<p>an extended lolFoundation joke</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I disagree. I can't help but feel that &quot;lolFoundation&quot; is a term that gets thrown around way too much to mean &quot;not 100% serious&quot;, when lolFoundation<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-613601-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-613601-1')">1</a></sup> means &quot;so incompetent that the Foundation could not feasibly operate in this manner&quot;. The Foundation here is absolutely serious and competent, only juxtaposing Hatbot's scrambled words and off-beat messages for comedic effect with the seriousness of the situation.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>and I'm not sure it can be fully understood without first having read the original &quot;Hatbot v Mann&quot; tale and Hello World.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>I don't think it's an article that works on its own merits.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Well, the contest is designed in a way where people are experiencing the best effects and easiest time by intertwining all the articles they make into a fluid story. Almost every single SCP posted thus far is part of a narrative, to where I think it's more jarring if an SCP is totally disconnected from every other article. It looks like a complete standalone from where I'm looking at it, that also happens to perfectly interconnect with other stories&#8230; same with almost every single SCP I've seen posted thus far.</p>
<p>I guess my personal gripe with this criticism as well is that it feels very silly to insist that all articles not explicitly labeled to be part of a series needs to standalone, let alone SCP articles. Let alone contest articles.</p>
